-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Worldforged pickups: what the first pass could not place
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2026_09_16_00_worldforged_pickups.sql spawns each pickup at one position - the
-- single example position the archive holds for that object, and an object the
-- first pass's rule could not see at all gets no object at all. Three things
-- follow from that, and this migration closes each one.
--
--  1. A pickup stands in every zone it was seen in, not one. The community's zone
--     dumps record the object's own XYZ zone by zone; an object the realm had in
--     several zones ended up only in the zone its example position fell in.
--  2. Seven pickups stand on the wrong map. Their position came from the two
--     region dumps (`dump_Azeroth.txt`, `dump_Kalimdor.txt`), whose map id is a
--     fallback and wrong for those rows: the zone dump records the same object at
--     the same coordinates on Kalimdor, and so does a pin of the object's own
--     item. A pickup on the wrong map is a pickup nobody finds.
--  3. A pickup whose position no zone dump holds gets no second spawn. The dumps
--     are one corpus, seen zone by zone in the open world; the archive's pins are
--     another, and they reach inside a dungeon, a mine, a cave, a starter sub-zone
--     or a TBC area the dumps never cover - a pin carries the object's own world
--     XYZ and the map it stands on. Where both corpora hold the same object they
--     agree to a median 27 yd, so a pin with no spawn within 30 yd is a position
--     nothing else records. None of the items these pickups hold exists anywhere
--     else in the database (no creature loot, no reference loot, no vendor, no
--     quest reward, no container), so a pin of such an item is a pickup and
--     nothing else.
--
-- WHERE EVERY VALUE COMES FROM
--   position   the community zone dump or the archive's pin, whichever records the
--              object - both are the object's own stored XYZ
--   height     the dump row of the same object when one stands within 60 yd of the
--              pin, else the nearest object the realm already has on that map
--   zone       the dump file / the pin's zone, which is what places the copy there
--   item       the first pass's own loot row for an object it already created; for
--              the new object, the pin of the realm within 3 yd of its position
--   fields     the client cache's template row, field for field, for the new object
--
-- No object is placed twice: a copy is added only where no spawn of the same object
-- already stands on that map within 30 yd, so the border zones that record one
-- object under two names produce one pickup, not two.
--
-- orientation/rotation: no source holds the realm's own facing, so each spawn
-- carries a stable per-object-and-position facing and the matching unit quaternion,
-- exactly as the first pass does - a re-import reproduces the same world.
--
-- Idempotent: the map corrections are UPDATEs on their own guids, the spawn block
-- is cleared by its own guid range first, and the new object is written with
-- REPLACE INTO (keyed on entry, and on Entry+Item for its loot row, carrying the
-- same AscensionWorldforged: comment prefix the first pass's own cleanup deletes
-- by). Apply to acore_world.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

START TRANSACTION;

-- The region dumps' map id is a fallback, and these carry the wrong one. The zone
-- dump records the same object at the same coordinates on Kalimdor, and a pin of
-- the object's own item is there too, so the map is corrected rather than
-- duplicated.
UPDATE `gameobject` SET `map` = 1,
    `Comment` = CONCAT(`Comment`, ' | map corrected to the zone dump''s own')
WHERE `guid` IN (6900366, 6900561, 6900698, 6900703, 6900724, 6900770, 6900779);

-- The pickup object the first pass's rule could not see: the realm's own catalog
-- holds no loot row for it, so no item pointed at it, but a pin of the item it
-- holds stands within 3 yd of it in six zones.


-- 2299 spawns: the pickups that stand in a zone, dungeon, mine, cave or TBC area the
-- first pass placed the object in only once - or never, for the object above.
DELETE FROM `gameobject` WHERE `guid` BETWEEN 6903001 AND 6909999;

COMMIT;
